{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>יְבִיאֶהָ אֵת תְּרוּמַת ה׳.</b> This verse points with a finger that alongside the tangible there is the intelligible — and it is their saying, of blessed memory (Tikkunim, Tikkun 21), that the act of the terumah is intelligible, being the aspect of the Shekhinah, for it is called terumat HaShem, and through the act of the terumah one activates in it a goodly matter and binds 'a tent that shall not fall,' and the principal intent in the act is the intelligible. And this is what it says — <b>יְבִיאֶהָ</b>, meaning, he shall bring [it] to the hidden aspect together with terumat HaShem. And the verse explained who it is that shall bring it, and said <b>כֹּל נְדִיב לִבּוֹ</b> — meaning, one who does a thing out of the elevation of the heart that impels him toward the spiritual stirring that dwells in the heart, with passion for the matter, will activate in the hidden intelligible; but if one brings the terumah without nedivut of heart, this does not effect anything in the intelligible." "<b>זָהָב וָכֶסֶף וְגוֹ׳.</b> I have seen fit to pay attention throughout the entire parashah to the order of the combination of the names and the details of the matters, for He did not make His measures uniform among them. Sometimes it says a vav for every additional detail — as it did in the order of the thirteen items that come in the donation of the Mishkan, where it said a vav for every addition — and sometimes it says a vav in the combination of three, as in its saying ״אֶת הַמִּשְׁכָּן אֶת אָהֳלוֹ וְאֶת מִכְסֵהוּ״, and immediately the verse itself changed course and took another order of combination and said ״אֶת קְרָסָיו וְאֶת קְרָשָׁיו״, and so on in this manner to the end of the order of the details of the Mishkan — many variations in this detail." "<b>וְנִרְאֶה</b> to resolve by prefacing their words, of blessed memory (Shemot Rabbah 30:3), that the vav indicates the binding of the additional to the first, and the absence of the vav indicates that it is a boundary unto itself. From this we will gain understanding concerning each and every matter and proceed to resolve each detail in its place. And this emerged first in the donation of the Mishkan: it said a vav at the head of every detail in order to join and equate all thirteen items, to say that all of them are equal in being called terumat HaShem, and their merit is the same — one who brings copper is like one who brings gold. As we find that the verse said concerning the animal olah ״רֵיחַ נִיחוֹחַ״ and concerning the bird olah ״רֵיחַ נִיחוֹחַ״, and they said, of blessed memory (Menachot 110a), to tell you — whether one who gives much or one who gives little, etc. And see what I explained on the matter in parashat Vayikra (Leviticus 1:17)." "Also, it further intends to say that all thirteen items are obligatory for the needs of the Mishkan — the small matter as the great one are indispensable; therefore it joined them with the bond of the vavim. All this is in the regard of the given item; but in the regard of what is made from them, there are gradations of holiness among them — how many degrees of holiness one is above the other, and one should not imagine any level of holiness to be like that which is above it."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:36:56.233270966Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}